## California Lemon Law Coverage for Pre-Owned Vehicles

California lemon law is not only for new vehicles. It also extends to pre-owned vehicles when sold with a warranty.

## How Do Warranties on Used Vehicles from Dealerships Work?

Used car dealerships in California often sell vehicles with a dealer warranty. The warranty is typically valid for 30 days after the purchase date or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer. You might be eligible for a claim under the California lemon law if your vehicle develops a defect under the terms of this warranty.

What Can You Claim Under California Lemon Law

Under the California lemon law, the dealerships are required to repair any defects covered under the warranty. If they fail to do so, you may be able to file a claim for a buyback. You can seek the following damages in your claim:

The cost of the vehicle

Official expenses _associated_ with the vehicle

Incidental costs

All legal and attorney fees

What Does Buying a Vehicle “As Is” Mean?

In California, when you buy a vehicle “[as is](https://www.autotrader.com/car-shopping/buying-a-used-car-what-does-as-is-mean-228533),” you agree to take on any problems it might run into after the purchase. This potentially reduces your coverage under the California lemon law.

However, for an “as is” sale to hold up legally, the dealership needs to explicitly say so on the buyer’s guide when showcasing the vehicle. If the dealership fails to properly reveal this information, you might have a claim under the California lemon law.

